course: biology
lesson: 60
title: Trees
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A tree is a plant that keeps building on itself year after year: every summer it adds a new layer of wood just under the bark, so a tree trunk is like a diary written in rings.
explain: Count the rings on a stump in the woods and you know its age: each year makes one ring, pale wood from fast spring growth and darker wood from slower late summer; a wide ring was a good year, a thin one a hard year.
explain: Trees are the record breakers of the living world: bristlecone pines over 4800 years old, a redwood called Hyperion about 116 metres tall, and a single oak that is home to more than 2000 kinds of living things.
explain: Most of a tree is made from the air: it takes in carbon dioxide, keeps the carbon in its wood and gives out oxygen, which is why old forests matter so much for the climate.
explain: Cartoons show trees as lonely and silent; under the ground their roots are joined to fungi that swap water and minerals for sugar, and some scientists think trees may share food this way, though how much is still argued about.
fact: Each year a tree grows a new ring of wood under its bark; scientists use tree rings to learn about the weather hundreds or thousands of years ago.
fact: Some Great Basin bristlecone pines in California are over 4800 years old; they grow very slowly in a cold, dry place where few pests survive, and slow-grown wood is dense and tough.
fact: The tallest known living tree is a coast redwood called Hyperion, about 116 metres tall, and the giant sequoia General Sherman is the biggest by volume; much taller is hard for any tree, because lifting water up to the top leaves gets too hard.
fact: Bark protects the living wood from insects, disease, fire and cold, and under it a thin living layer makes new wood and bark; cutting a ring of bark all round can kill a tree.
fact: Cork comes from the thick bark of the cork oak, peeled about every 9 years without killing the tree.
fact: An oak gall forms when a tiny wasp lays an egg in an oak bud and the tree grows a ball round it; for centuries people made black ink from oak galls.
fact: Pine cones open their scales in dry weather to let winged seeds blow away, since seeds travel best on dry windy days, and close in damp air and rain; some open only after a forest fire.
fact: Deciduous trees drop their leaves in autumn and protect next year's tiny leaves and flowers inside buds wrapped in tough scales; horse chestnut buds are glued shut and sticky, and conkers are poisonous to eat.
fact: Beech trees make a thick roof of leaves, so little grows beneath them except plants like bluebells that flower in spring before the beech leaves open.
fact: Many tree roots are joined to fungi, a link sometimes called the «wood wide web»; the mushrooms are only the fruit of a much bigger fungus underground, and wild mushrooms must never be picked or eaten as some are deadly.
fact: The baobab stores large amounts of water in its spongy trunk to survive months without rain, drops its leaves in the dry season so it does not lose water through them, and some baobabs are over 1000 years old.
fact: Rainforests cover only about 6 percent of the land but hold more than half of all the kinds of land plants and animals, and most of the animals live up in the canopy, where most of the fruit, flowers and leaves are.
exercise: How can you tell the age of a tree from its stump? | Count the rings, one for each year | Measure how wide the stump is in metres | Count the branches on the stump | Look at the colour of the bark
exercise: What does a wide tree ring tell you? | That was a good growing year | The tree was very ill that year | The tree was pruned hard that year | There was a forest fire that year
exercise: About how old are the oldest bristlecone pines? | Over 4800 years | About 480 years old | About 48 years old | Over 48000 years
exercise: Why do bristlecone pines live so long? | They grow slowly in a cold, dry place where few pests survive | They are watered and looked after every single week by the park rangers | They drink sea water | They grow very fast and never stop
exercise: About how tall is Hyperion, the tallest known living tree? | About 116 metres | About 16 metres tall | About 1160 metres | About 60 metres tall
exercise: Why is an oak's bark rough and thick? | It protects the living wood from insects, disease, fire and cold | It helps the oak catch rain and pour it down the trunk to its roots | It is old dead leaves stuck to the trunk | It is where the tree makes its food
exercise: What is an oak gall? | A ball the tree grows round a tiny wasp's egg | An acorn that has gone bad and turned hard and brown | A bird's nest made of bark | A fungus that kills oaks
exercise: Why do pine cones open on a sunny day and close in the rain? | Seeds travel best on dry windy days, so the scales open only then | Cones are scared of water | The hot sun melts the sticky resin holding the scales together | Squirrels open them in sunshine
exercise: What is inside a horse chestnut's sticky bud in March? | Tiny new leaves and flowers, kept safe from frost | A small conker waiting to grow and fall | A sleeping insect | Glue for the bark, ready for the summer
exercise: Why does little grow on the ground in a beech wood in summer? | The thick roof of beech leaves lets little light through | Beech trees send out poison from their roots that kills all other plants | Deer eat everything in beech woods | The soil there is made of sand
exercise: What are the mushrooms coming up in a ring round a tree? | The fruit of a big fungus living underground with the roots | Tiny new trees growing up out of the roots of the old one in a circle | Eggs laid by beetles | Seeds dropped by the tree
exercise: Why does a baobab have a huge fat trunk? | It stores water to survive months without rain | It is hollow so that animals can live inside it | It is full of air to stay light | It is made of solid stone
exercise: What does a tree do with the carbon dioxide it takes in? | Stores the carbon in its wood and gives out oxygen | Breathes it straight back out again, unchanged, at night | Sends it down into the soil as water | Turns it into smoke
exercise: How much of the land do rainforests cover? | About 6 percent | About half of them | About 90 percent | Less than one in a thousand
exercise: Where do most animals in the rainforest live? | Up in the canopy | On the forest floor | Underground among the roots | In the forest rivers
exercise: Why can cutting a ring of bark all round a tree kill it? | It cuts the living layer and tubes that carry food round the tree | The tree gets cold and catches flu | Insects get in and eat all the leaves at once, from the top down | The tree loses its grip on the ground
test: What might a very thin tree ring tell you about that year? | It was a hard year, perhaps dry or cold | It was the tree's birthday | The tree grew the fastest it ever has | Someone painted a thin line on the tree that year
test: Why might growing slowly help a tree live longer? | Slow wood is dense and tough, and the harsh place keeps pests away | Slow trees sleep more | Slow trees are protected by law and nobody is allowed to cut them down | Growing fast makes trees explode
test: What would make it hard for a tree to grow much taller than Hyperion? | Lifting water so high up to the top leaves gets too hard | The clouds would push it down | Birds would not land on it | The bark would get far too heavy for the trunk to hold up any longer
test: If your friend reckons the big redwood in the photo is the biggest tree in the world by volume, what do you say? | Not quite: Hyperion is tallest, but General Sherman is biggest | Yes, the tallest is always the biggest, that's how it works | No, the biggest tree is an oak in England | No, all redwoods are the same size
test: Why can cork be taken from a cork oak again and again? | Only the thick outer bark is peeled, and the living layer grows more | The cork oak grows a whole new trunk each year after the old one | It can't, the tree dies after one peel | Cork comes from the roots, not the bark
test: Why would one oak tree be home for over 2000 kinds of living things? | It is big, lives for centuries and offers leaves, bark, acorns and holes | Oaks give out a sweet smell that pulls in animals from miles around | Animals are not allowed in other trees | All 2000 kinds eat only acorns
test: How could you use a pine cone to tell the weather? | Open scales mean dry air, closed scales mean damp air | It turns blue before it rains | It falls off the tree when snow is on the way, a day early | It ticks like a clock in the sun
test: in that cartoon the trees are lonely and don't talk at all. true? | Roots are linked by fungi that trade water and food, though how much is argued | Trees chat using sounds we can't hear, and it has all been proven by scientists | Yes, trees have no links at all with anything | Trees send messages through their leaves to the clouds
test: Why do bluebells flower early in spring in a beech wood? | They bloom before the beech leaves open and block out the light | They like the cold better than the warm | The bees only come out in March, and the bluebells must catch them | Beech trees tell them when to flower
test: Why is it important to keep old forests as well as plant new trees? | Old forests hold lots of carbon and are home to many living things | New trees are always better than old ones | Old trees make no oxygen at all | It isn't, one new tree does just the same job as a whole old forest
test: Why are so many rainforest animals good at climbing or flying? | Most of the fruit, flowers and leaves are up in the canopy | The forest floor is covered in deep water all year, so nothing can walk there | They are scared of the rain | Climbing keeps them warm
test: Why might a baobab drop its leaves in the dry season? | To stop losing water through its leaves when there is no rain | Because the leaves are too heavy | Because the animals have eaten them all | To let more sunlight in to dry out and harden the trunk
exam: Identify the information most reliably conveyed by the annual rings of a tree. | The age of the tree and the character of each growing season | The number of animals inhabiting the tree | The height to which the tree will ultimately grow in its lifetime | The quantity of water presently stored in the trunk
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the opening of the scales of a pine cone? | The scales open in dry weather, permitting the dispersal of winged seeds | The scales open in wet weather to admit rain | The scales open only when a squirrel strikes the cone with sufficient force | The scales open nightly to release pollen
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the so-called wood wide web. | Roots are joined to fungi, though how far trees share food is disputed | It is a proven network by which trees converse in speech | It is a system of wires installed by foresters | It denotes the branches of adjacent trees touching overhead
exam: Ascertain the proportion of the land surface of the Earth covered by tropical rainforests. | Approximately six per cent | Approximately one half | Approximately ninety per cent | Approximately one per thousand
